"Rebecca's writing is phenomenal,&#8221; said Eleazar. &#8220;Nuanced and real. I am drawn to stories that live in the grey area of life, where multiple truths can exist. Wren is a deeply conflicted woman, trying to navigate her life, impacted by consequences from her past. The prospect of working with John has long been a dream of mine and so bringing this story to life with this team is super exciting."

Malkovich is the two-time Academy Award-nominated actor, producer and director and a founding member of Chicago's Steppenwolf Theatre Company. He's currently starring in Martin McDonagh's Wild Horse Nine . Other West End credits include A Slip of the Tongue and Bitter Wheat, among others. On Broadway he's appeared in Death of a Salesman, Burn This and Arms and the Man.

Malkovich is currently in production on the Amazon Prime thriller series Bishop .

Eleazar is best known for playing Louisa Guy in Slow Horses , now in production for an upcoming seventh season. She currently stars in A24's Misty Green , and has appeared on stage in Small Hotel, Uncle Vanya, The Starry Messenger and others. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dl-mid-article-inject2-uid1' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article2","mid-articleX","mid-article","mid","300x251"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article2") .addSize([[300,250],[300,251],[620,350],[2,4],[4,2],[620,366]]) .exemptFromSleep() .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

&#8220;John brings the heft of greatness,&#8221; director Dunster said. &#8220;Rosalind is electrifying on stage and screen. I feel very lucky to be working with this team on Rebecca's play."

Irish actress, writer and producer Sharon Horgan will be the guest of honor at the 2027 CanneSeries Industry, the international TV event held alongside the CanneSeries television festival, organizers announced Tuesday.

CanneSeries called Horgan, a BAFTA winner and Emmy nominee for her work on such series as Catastrophe , Divorce and Bad Sisters , an &#8220;iconic voice&#8221; of international TV and &#8220;undoubtedly one of the wittiest and funniest of our time.&#8221;

In a statement, Albin Lewi and Claire-Marine Pietriga, artistic director and deputy artistic director of CanneSeries praised Horgan for her &#8220;sharp writing, dark humor and unflinching portrayal of human relationships,&#8221; noting her ability to &#8220;seamlessly blend comedy and drama, bringing to life complex, flawed and deeply authentic female characters.&#8221; blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-article-mid-article-uid0' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article1","mid-articleX","mid","mid-article"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article") .addSize([[300,250],[2,2],[300,251],[620,350],[2,4],[4,2],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450],[4,4],[300,251]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; }); Related Stories Movies 'You Can See Everything' Cinematographer on Billy Evans' Nude Scene and Tension Between Him and the Team General News From Westeros and Venice Festival Winner 'Woman Unknown' to 'Star City': Meet Baltic Craft Specialists Who've Worked on Int'l Productions

As the co-founder of the production company Merman, they added &#8220;she continues to push the boundaries of television storytelling, championing bold narratives driven by strong female perspectives.&#8221;

Horgan will attend CanneSeries Industry and will take part in its "meet with the participants&#8221; program to reflect on the key milestones of her career.

The third edition of CanneSeries Industry will be held from Feb. 15-17 alongside the 10th CanneSeries festival, which runs Feb. 12-17. CanneSeries was initially a Spring event, held alongside the now-defunct TV trade fair MIPTV. It is shifting to February next year, putting it ahead of rival French TV event Series Mania, which runs March 19-26.

How far have the Country Music Association Awards come from their inception in 1967 to this year's 60th annual telecast? Outgoing CMA CEO Sarah Trahern , who retires on Dec. 31, has a better idea than most. When she first took the reins of the leadership toward the end of 2014, she dug into the organization's archives to find DVDs that had been kept of the 46 CMA Awards shows that had gone down up to that point, and binge-watched them over the holidays. (There was one gap, the 1969 show, which was lost to history.) blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-variety-article-mid-article-uid0' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article1","mid-articleX","mid","mid-article"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article") .addSize([[300,250],[2,2],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; }); Related Stories Emile Hirsch to Star in Texas-Set Thriller 'The Violet Crown' (EXCLUSIVE)

&#8220;It was fun to watch the really early shows with Johnny Cash hosting, where you would see him take a drag on his cigarette during the commercial breaks,&#8221; she laughs. Smoking aside, those were more innocent advertorial days. &#8220;We were sponsored by Kraft in the first years, and they would toss it at the break to someone to do a macaroni and cheese recipe, or what you can put on your Thanksgiving table. So I was watching that like, &#8216;Man, we've come a long way.'&#8221; Watching the old telecast DVDs, she also caught up on some of the shows' more legendary moments. &#8220;I had never before seen the famous moment in 1975 when Charlie Rich, who was presenting for Entertainer of the Year, burned the envelope when John Denver won.&#8221; Rich's open diss of Denver represented a tension that hasn't completely gone away since then. &#8220;You know,&#8221; Trahern muses, &#8220;it's appropriate that every now and then things move to pop and the traditionalists get upset.&#8221;

Where are we in that dynamic in 2026? In kind of a pleasantly strange place where there is a very traditional-sounding country song, Ella Langley's &#8220;Choosin' Texas,&#8221; that happens to be a pop crossover smash, and the biggest all-genre song of the year, by any measure. On top of the record-breaking 25-week run that &#8220;Choosin' Texas&#8221; has had atop the Billboard Hot 100, the song is inevitably the 500-pound gorilla in this year's CMA nods, which sees Langley going into the telecast with a leading nine nominations. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dsk-tab-mid-article2-uid1' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid","mid-article2","mid-articleX"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article2") .addSize([[320,480],[300,250],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

Fortunately, Langley is no one-woman pack, but just the current leading edge of a wave of women that have dominated the awards in recent years. &#8220;what a wonderful year that Ella has had, and we're all proud to be able to be on her coattails as she's had growth all around the world,&#8221; Trahern says &#x2014; &#8220;in the same way that Lainey Wilson has really stepped out as our host, and we're so excited she's back for the third year. The fact that we have these monster female artists in the format right now is exciting. &#8220;So many of our top nominated people, from Ella to Kacey (Musgraves), are marching to their own drummers and writing their own stuff and taking it to different eras. To have Miranda (Lambert) showing up not just as an artist and female vocalist, but as a producer and writer too (for her work on Langley's recordings), is kind of exciting. And Taylor coming back,&#8221; with Swift, the world's biggest pop artist not only nominated for her return to a country sound with &#8220;I Knew It, I Knew You&#8221; but promising to not just be in attendance for the first time in a decade but perform on the telecast for the first time in 13 years.

The 60th annual show, which will air live on ABC and Disney+ and have next-day viewing on Hulu, has a lot to live up to. Trahern can think of a lot more highlights from the telecast's 21st century airings that she did not have to go back to the archival DVDs to catch up on.

&#8220;The first time I got to go sit in the house for the CMA Awards was 2001 when Alan Jackson did &#8216;Where Were You (When the World Stopped Turning?),' and anytime I hear that song, I get transported back to the Opry House that night and all of our jaws dropping at the power of that performance.&#8221; That song remains the best-remembered 9/11 memorial in any genre. She adds, &#8220;I felt that same emotion when we did the Route 91 tribute with Carrie Underwood&#8221; in 2017, following the gun massacre at a festival in Las Vegas. On that occasion, Underwood sang the hymn &#8220;Softly and Tenderly,&#8221; captured in a single steadicam shot, with photos of the country fans who perished in the tragedy on screens behind her.

&#8220;Those are certainly those poignant memories, but I also think about rocking, up-tempo moments,&#8221; Trahern continues. She singles out the collaboration of Beyonc&#xE9; and the Chicks in 2016, when they did a mashup of &#8220;Daddy Issues&#8221; and &#8220;Long Time Gone.&#8221; As collabs go, that one might be rivaled only by the 2015 duet between Justin Timberlake and Chris Stapleton of &#8220;Tennessee Whiskey&#8221; that made the latter up-and-comer an overight sensation, in one of the few instances in which we can use that term literally. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-variety-article-mid-articleX-uid2' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["btf","mid-articleX","mid"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-articleX") .addSize([[320,480],[300,250],[300,251],[620,366]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

She can cite star-making appearances from the '90s and the present day in a single breath. &#8220;I think of Stephen Wilson Jr.'s performance last year, when he did &#8216;Stand By Me' just with his guitar, one of those moments when people kinda paid attention in a different way and went, &#8216; Oh. &#8216; Or I think back to Mary Chapin Carpenter in the '90s, when she barely had a couple of songs out when she did &#8216;The Opening Act,' and the parlor game in the industry immediately became: &#8216;Who's it about?' Everyone could laugh and relate to having that story happen to them with some artist. That's the magic of a show like this where someone can come out of the blue.&#8221;

A strong, spontaneous-seeming speech can historically have almost as much impact as a great performance. Trahern recalls &#8220;the time that Taylor won Horizon [aka best new artist], and said it was a highlight of her senior year. Or when Deana Carter jumped into Ricky Skaggs' lap. Or Zach Top taking his beer up on stage last year because he forgot he had it. There are those pop culture moments you can't predict till they're happening in the show. But the musical stuff &#x2014; Alan; Beyonc&#xE9; and the Chicks; Stapleton and JT &#x2014; those are the things that I really hold dear.&#8221; There's a pause, and then a promise she makes, as a quick, modest aside: &#8220;We'll have a couple of those this year.&#8221;

Nov. 18 will mark the last time Trahern oversees the airing of the CMA Awards, but this year will also mark a significant first. It's the first time the show will be simulcast on a streaming service, as well as its home base on ABC. That might seem overdue, but it's the outgrowth of the association reupping its deal with Disney for 2027 and beyond, and then getting a head start on some of its provisions.

&#8220;We had the opportunity this year to talk to a lot of different networks in our negotiation process, and when we set out on that at the beginning of the year, some of my goals were to make sure that our new long-term TV partnership met where the industry is going,&#8221; Trahern says. That didn't involve anything so extreme as the rival ACM Awards making an exclusive deal with Amazon Prime. But &#8220;having a simulcast in a way that we can reach both the traditional viewers and the streaming viewers was number one on our negotiation team's list. We didn't necessarily know where that was gonna lead us, but to be back in the Disney family with ABC for our linear and Disney+ for the simulcast is particularly exciting. That deal that we negotiated starts next year, but we're able to pull that forward so that we can stream, or simulcast, for the first time this year. That unlocks a whole new generation of viewers who don't have linear television at home, and we're really excited with this new partnership and to be able to bring the show to millions of new and ideally younger fans who are consuming music today.&#8221; blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-variety-article-mid-articleX-uid3' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["btf","mid-articleX","mid"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-articleX") .addSize([[320,480],[300,250],[300,251],[620,366]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

One issue they faced specifically looking ahead to this year's telecast was the Dolly of it all. The CMAs had a potentially difficult dance in front of them, with how to salute her on the Nov. 18 show. Devote anything less than about 45 minutes of the three hours to eulogizing Dolly Parton, and folks would complain. And perhaps they still will. But the org and ABC preempted at least some of that by giving Parton her own separate two-hour tribute special on Oct. 21.

